      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "When the Louisville & Nashville Railroad finished in 1859, Oakland sprang up as a boomtown, shipping tobacco, grain and livestock from 1870 to 1900.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://oakland.ky.gov/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A fire in the early twentieth century destroyed most of Oakland’s commercial district, but the City Hall, Post Office, General Store and bank building—now a Free Mason Lodge—remain.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://oakland.ky.gov/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Oakland and the nearby Freeport were jointly listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 2004, recognizing their well‑preserved 19th‑century architecture.",
